right. Wait, second job's rate is 20% less than the main job. So, actually, his second job rate is $20 times (1 - 20%) which is $20 times 0.8. So, 20% less. Let me think. If the main job is $20, 20% less would be 80% of that, right? So, 0.8 times 20. Which is 16. So, he earns $16 per hour at his second job.\n\nYes, that seems correct. So, the second job is 16 dollars per hour. Therefore, for 15 hours, that would be 15 times 16. Let me calculate that. 10 times 16 is 160, 5 times 16 is 80, so 160 + 80 equals 240. So, his earnings from the second job is $240.\n\nNow, adding the two parts together: $600 from the main job plus $240 from the second job. Let me add those. 600 + 240 equals 840. Therefore, his total earnings per week should be $840.\n\nWait, let me double-check my calculations again to be sure I didn't make any mistakes. First job: 30 x 20 = 600. Second job: 15 x 16. Let me compute 15 x 16 again. 15 times 10 is 150, 15 times 6 is 90, so total is 240. Yep, that's correct. Adding to 600: 600 + 240 = 840. So, $840 per week.\n\nAlternatively, I can also calculate the second job's earnings by considering the rate. The main job is 20%, so 20% of 20 is 4, so 80% of 20 is 16. Therefore, the second job is 16, so half of that? Wait, half of the hours. Wait, so if the second job rate is 80% of the main job rate, then per hour, it's $16. So, per hour he works 16, and he works 15 hours. So, 15 times 16 is 240. Which matches my previous result. So, total is 600 + 240 = 840. \n\nAnother way to think about it is total hours per week: 30 + (half of 30). Half of 30 is 15, so 45 hours total. But since James earns $20 per hour at the first job and a different rate at the second. But since he works 30 hours at his main job, and half that at his second job, which seems to be 15 hours. Therefore, adding those together: 30 * 20 + 15 * 16 = 600 + 240 = 840. Yep.\n\nI think that's solid. So, the answer should be $840.\n</think>\n\nJames earns $20 per hour at his main job, and while working his second job, he earns 20% less than that, meaning 80% of $20, or $16 per hour. He works 30 hours at the main job and half of that (15 hours) at the second job. To find his weekly earnings:\n\n1. Earnings from the main job:  \n   $30 \\, \\text{hours} \\times \\$20/\\text{hour} = \\$600$  \n\n2. Earnings from the second job:  \n   $15 \\, \\text{hours} \\times \\$16/\\text{hour} = \\$240$  \n\n3. Total weekly earnings:  \n   $ \\$600 + \\$240 = \\$840 $  \n\n$$\n\\boxed{840}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "James earns $20 an hour while working at his main job.  He earns 20% less while working his second job.  He works 30 hours at his main job and half that much at his second job.  How much does he earn per week?", "answer": "James earns 20*.2=$<<20*.2=4>>4 less while working his second job\nSo he earns 20-4=$<<20-4=16>>16 an hour\nAt his first job he earns 20*30=$<<20*30=600>>600\nHe works 30/2=<<30/2=15>>15 hours at his second job\nSo he earns 15*16=$<<15*16=240>>240\nSo he earns 600+240=$<<600+240=840>>840 a week\n#### 840", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: James earns $20 an hour while working at his main job.  He earns 20% less while working his second job.  He works 30 hours at his main job and half that much at his second job.  How much does he earn per week?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["840"], "solution": "840"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 7801, 63759, 400, 17, 15, 458, 6460, 1393, 3238, 518, 806, 1887, 2618, 13, 220, 1260, 63759, 220, 17, 15, 4, 2686, 1393, 3238, 806, 2086, 2618, 13, 220, 1260, 4278, 220, 18, 15, 4115, 518, 806], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 1077, 752, 1430, 311, 7071, 700, 1246, 1753, 7801, 63759, 817, 2003, 13, 88190, 11, 773, 566, 4278, 1378, 6887, 13, 5512, 11, 5