tween John and those 3. So total cost is divided by 4, John's share is total / 4.\n\nTherefore, total cost is 580, John's share is 580 / 4 = 145. \n\nBut earlier when I thought about splitting a group, if you have 4 people, each pays half. So maybe the problem here is that John is paying split, and others also pay. But maybe the problem is that splitting the cost with 3 other people means that John shares with them. Therefore, children contribute equally. \n\nAlternatively, maybe John is one person, and the other 3 are his friends. Then total people is 4. So total cost is divided between each person. Therefore, John pays one part, his two friends pay another part, and they? Wait, the problem says \"they buy 100 pounds...\" So maybe the total cost is that amount, and John splits it with 3 others.\n\nBut then, if the total is 580, splitting with 3 others would mean that John paid half? Or each person (including his 3 friends) paid all?\n\nThis is critical. Because if the problem says \"John splits the cost with 3 other people\", maybe they mean that John is paying, and each of his 3 friends pays. Therefore, total cost is divided by 4. Then John's share is 580 / 4 = 145. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is that John is paying, and the others also pay, meaning that there's no division. But that seems unlikely. \n\nAlternatively, maybe there was a translation issue. Perhaps \"split the cost\" means that John and his friends split each item's cost. But the problem says \"John splits the cost with 3 other people.\" \n\nAlternatively, maybe they buy the burgers, condiments, propane, and alcohol, and John splits the cost between his and his friends. So with 4 people, each pays 1/4. Therefore, John's share is 145. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is that the total cost is divided between John and his 3 friends, but the problem states he splits the cost with others. \n\nBut since the problem says \"John splits the cost with 3 other people\", and doesn't specify that th