ought 15, Diane buys 15 + 20 = 35. Then adding together 15 and 35 gives 50. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can think of it as a numbers problem. Let me write it out:\n\nCecile: 15 apples.\n\nDiane: 20 more than Cecile. So 15 + 20 = 35.\n\nTotal apples: 15 (Cecile) + 35 (Diane) = 50. \n\nYeah, that seems consistent. I don't see any mistakes in my reasoning here. So I think the answer is 50.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThey bought \\boxed{50} apples altogether.\n</think>\n\nTo find out how many apples Diane and Cecile bought altogether, we begin by using the information provided.\n\nWe are told:\n- Cecile bought **15 apples**.\n- Diane bought **20 more** apples than Cecile.\n\nThis means Diane’s number of apples is:\n$$\n15 + 20 = 35\n$$\n\nNow, we add the number of apples bought by both Diane and Cecile:\n$$\n15 \\text{ (Cecile’s apples)} + 35 \\text{ (Diane’s apples)} = 50\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Final Answer**\nThey bought \\boxed{50} apples altogether.<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Diane bought twenty more apples than Cecile. If Cecile bought 15 apples, how many apples did they buy altogether?", "answer": "Diane bought 15 + 20 = <<15+20=35>>35 apples.\nTherefore, they bought 15 + 35 = <<15+35=50>>50 apples altogether.\n#### 50", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Diane bought twenty more apples than Cecile. If Cecile bought 15 apples, how many apples did they buy altogether?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["50"], "solution": "50"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 53090, 10788, 17073, 803, 40676, 1091, 49145, 457, 13, 1416, 49145, 457, 10788, 220, 16, 20, 40676, 11, 1246, 1657, 40676, 1521, 807, 3695, 30055, 5267, 36842, 25, 151645, 198, 151644, 77091, 198], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 773, 53090, 10788, 17073, 803, 40676, 1091, 