2026-10-30 04:08:15 INFO areno.api.trainers.policy_only.PolicyOnlyTrainer policy_only.py:723 - rollout_completion={"kind": "rollout", "epoch": 0, "step": 20, "prompt_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Michelangelo has 28 ceilings to paint. This week, he paints 12 of them. Next week, he will paint 1/4 the number of ceilings he did this week. How many ceilings will be left to paint after next week?\nSolution:", "decoded_prompt": "<|im_start|>user\nSolve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Michelangelo has 28 ceilings to paint. This week, he paints 12 of them. Next week, he will paint 1/4 the number of ceilings he did this week. How many ceilings will be left to paint after next week?\nSolution:<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n", "completion": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. Michelangelo has 28 ceilings to paint in total. This week, he paints 12 of them. Then next week, he paints 1/4 of the number he did this week. I need to figure out how many ceilings will be left after next week. Hmm.\n\nFirst, let me confirm the numbers. Total ceilings: 28. Painted this week: 12. So, how many are left to paint after this week? Well, 28 minus 12 equals... 16. That's straightforward. So, there are 16 ceilings left.\n\nNow, next week, he will paint 1/4 of what he did this week. The problem says \"1/4 the number of ceilings he did this week.\" So, he will paint 1/4 of 12. Let me compute that. 12 divided by 4 is 3. So, next week he paints 3 ceilings.\n\nTo find out how many are left after next week, I need to subtract the number he painted next week from the remaining before next week. The remaining before next week is 16, and he paints 3. So, 16 minus 3 equals 13. Therefore, there will be 13 ceilings left to paint after next week.\n\nWait, let me double-check. Total ceilings: 28. Painted this week: 12. Left before next week: 28 - 12 = 16. Next week, he paints 1/4 of 12, which is 12/4 = 3. So, 16 - 3 = 13. Yeah, that seems right. \n\nIs there any chance I misread the problem? Let me check again. The question is about remaining ceilings after next week. So, first week: 28 - 12 = 16. Then, next week: 1/4 of that. Wait, does the problem specify that he paints 1/4 of the number he did this week? Yes, it says next week, he will paint 1/4 the number of ceilings he did this week. So, 1/4 of 12 is 3. So, subtract 3 from 16. Yep, 13. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can think of it as total ceilings minus the ones painted both weeks. But since he painted this week and then next week, we need to subtract both amounts. So, starting with 28, subtract 12, which leaves 16. Then, subtract 3 more. 16 - 3 = 13. Yep, same answer. \n\nI think that's correct. There's no trick here, just basic arithmetic. No fractions involved unless it's about division after subtraction, which it is, but the question is straightforward. \n\nSo, the answer should be 13. \n\n**Final Answer**\nThe number of ceilings left to paint is \\boxed{13}.\n</think>\n\nTo solve the problem, we start by analyzing the given information.\n\n**Total ceilings to paint:** 28\n**Ceilings painted this week:** 12\n\n**Step 1: Calculate the number of ceilings left to paint before next week.**\n\n$$\n28 - 12 = 16\n$$\n\n**Step 2: Determine how many ceilings Michelangelo will paint next week.**\n\nHe paints $ \\frac{1}{4} $ of the number of ceilings he painted this week. \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{4} \\times 12 = 3\n$$\n\n**Step 3: Calculate the number of ceilings left to paint after next week.**\n\n$$\n16 - 3 = 13\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Final Answer**\n\nThe number of ceilings left to paint after next week is:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{13}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Michelangelo has 28 ceilings to paint. This week, he paints 12 of them. Next week, he will paint 1/4 the number of ceilings he did this week. How many ceilings will be left to paint after next week?", "answer": "After this week, Michelangelo will have 28-12=<<28-12=16>>16 more ceilings to paint.\nNext week, Michelangelo will paint 12/4=<<12/4=3>>3 ceilings.\nMichelangelo will have 16-3=<<16-3=13>>13 more ceilings to paint.\n#### 13", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Michelangelo has 28 ceilings to paint. This week, he paints 12 of them. Next week, he will paint 1/4 the number of ceilings he did this week. How many ceilings will be left to paint after next week?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["13"], "solution": "13"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 44406, 87910, 702, 220, 17, 23, 55714, 311, 6177, 13, 1096, 2003, 11, 566, 56848, 220, 16, 17, 315, 1105, 13, 9295, 2003, 11, 566, 686, 6177, 220, 16, 14, 19, 279, 1372, 315, 55714, 566, 1521], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 1077, 594, 1490, 13, 44406, 87910, 702, 220, 17, 23, 55714, 311, 6177, 304, 2790, 13, 1096, 2003, 11, 566, 56848, 220, 16, 17, 315, 1105, 13, 5005, 1790, 2003, 11, 566, 56848, 220, 16, 14, 19, 315, 279, 1372, 566, 1521, 419, 2003, 13, 358, 1184, 311, 7071, 700, 1246, 1657, 55714, 686, 387, 2115, 1283, 1790, 2003, 13]}
