alculate that. 70 times $2 is $140, and 0.5 bags is half a dollar, so that's $1. So total cost is $140 + $1 = $141.\n\nTherefore, Harris will spend $141 on carrots in one year. Let me double-check my reasoning. Total carrots per year is 365. Number of bags is 365 divided by 5 per bag, which gives 73 bags. Wait, hold on, earlier I got 70.5 bags. That was because 5 times 70 is 350, 5 times 71 is 355. So 365 is 15 more than 355, so 0.5 more bags. So same thing. So 0.5 times $2 is $1. So total $141. Yes, that seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, if I think in terms of per month, maybe a different number, but since the problem says a year, 365 days is correct. So I think my answer is right. So the total cost is $141.\n\n**Final Answer**\nHarris will spend \\boxed{141} dollars on carrots in one year.\n</think>\n\nTo solve the problem, we start by determining how many total carrots Harris will feed each year. Since the dog consumes 1 carrot per day, and a year has approximately 365 days, we calculate:\n\n$$\n\\text{Total number of carrots/year} = 365 \\, \\text{carrots/day} \\times 1 \\, \\text{day/year} = 365 \\, \\text{carrots}\n$$\n\nNext, we figure out how many bags of carrots Harris needs. A bag contains 5 carrots, so we perform:\n\n$$\n\\text{Number of bags} = \\frac{365}{5} = 73\n$$\n\nSince 1 bag costs $2, we determine the total cost as:\n\n$$\n\\text{Cost} = 73 \\, \\text{bags} \\times 2 \\, \\text{\\$/bag} = 146 \\, \\text{\\$}\n$$\n\nHowever, this calculation assumes 73 bags, which is an exact value. But because 0.5 of a bag is still meaningful when working with 0.5×$2 = $1, we must consider that as:\n\n$$\n\\text{Cost} = 70 \\, \\text{bags} \\times 2 + 0.5 \\, \\text{bags} \\times 2 = 140 + 1 = 141\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total cost for Harris's yearly feeding of his dog is:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{141}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Harris feeds his dog 1 large organic carrot over the course of 1 day.  There are 5 carrots in a 1 pound bag and each bag costs $2.00.  In one year, how much will Harris spend on carrots?", "answer": "His dog gets 1 carrot a day and there are 365 days in a year, so his dog eats 1*365 = <<1*365=365>>365 carrots\nThere are 5 carrots per bag and he will need 365 carrots, so that's 365/5 = 73 bags of carrots\nThe bags cost $2.00 and he will need 73 bags so that's $2*73 = $<<2*73=146.00>>146.00 worth of organic carrots\n#### 146", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Harris feeds his dog 1 large organic carrot over the course of 1 day.  There are 5 carrots in a 1 pound bag and each bag costs $2.00.  In one year, how much will Harris spend on carrots?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["146"], "solution": "146"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 21071, 34396, 806, 5562, 220, 16, 3460, 17356, 74194, 916, 279, 3308, 315, 220, 16, 1899, 13, 220, 2619, 525, 220, 20, 61417, 304, 264, 220, 16, 30023, 8968, 323, 1817, 8968, 7049, 400, 17, 13, 15], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 1077, 594, 1490, 13, 21071, 34396, 806, 5562, 220, 16, 3460, 17356, 74194, 817, 1899, 13, 2055, 11, 429, 3363, 1817, 1899, 11, 566, 3880, 220, 16, 74194, 13, 576, 3491, 374, 10161, 1246, 1753, 566, 3278, 8329, 389, 61417, 304, 825, 1042, 11, 1290, 1939, 5338, 11, 358, 1184, 311, 7071, 700, 1246, 1657, 2849, 525, 304, 264, 1042]}
