is exactly 10,000. \n\nAlternatively, maybe think in terms of fractions. Each balloon requires 10 liters, so 1000 balloons need 10 liters per balloon times 1000. Then, dividing by 500 gives the number of tanks. Yep, same result. \n\nI don't see any mistakes here. The answer should be 20 tanks.\n</think>\n\nTo determine how many 500-liter gas tanks John needs to buy to fill all 1000 balloons, we follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate total air required**:\n   - Each balloon holds 10 liters of air.\n   - There are 1000 balloons.\n   - $ 1000 \\times 10 = 10,000 $ liters of air.\n\n2. **Determine the number of 500-liter tanks needed**:\n   - Each tank holds 500 liters.\n   - Divide the total liters needed by the volume per tank: $ \\frac{10,000}{500} = 20 $.\n\nThus, John needs to buy **20** tanks of gas.\n\n$$\n\\boxed{20}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "John buys 1000 balloons.  Each balloon holds 10 liters of air.  If he buys 500-liter tanks of gas, how many tanks does he need to buy to fill all the balloons?", "answer": "He needs 1000*10=<<1000*10=10000>>10000 liters of gas\nSo he needs 10000/500=<<10000/500=20>>20 tanks\n#### 20", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: John buys 1000 balloons.  Each balloon holds 10 liters of air.  If he buys 500-liter tanks of gas, how many tanks does he need to buy to fill all the balloons?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["20"], "solution": "20"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 3757, 49531, 220, 16, 15, 15, 15, 69480, 13, 220, 8886, 46756, 9982, 220, 16, 15, 92866, 315, 3720, 13, 220, 1416, 566, 49531, 220, 20, 15, 15, 85594, 25866, 315, 6819, 11, 1246, 1657, 25866, 1558], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 1077, 594, 1490, 13, 3757, 49531, 220, 16, 15, 15, 15, 69480, 13, 8886, 46756