 300 + (5 * 20) students. Calculating that: 5*20 is 100, so total students would be 300 + 100 = 400 students. So the answer should be 400. \n\nWait, but let me confirm once more. When they say they added five more classes, does that mean that each of those five classes has 20 students? So each of the 20 classes has 20 students, so total students would be 20 classes * 20 students/class = 400. Yeah, that's the same result. So 400 students. \n\nI think that's it. Let me check if there's any trick or anything. The problem states that they added five more classes, so the total number of classes increases. But the initial 15 classes and 20 each. So no, there's no mention of any scaling or other factors. The problem seems straightforward. Therefore, the answer should be 400.\n</think>\n\nTo solve the problem, we begin by determining the total number of students after adding five new classes to the existing 15 classes.\n\n- The school currently has **15 classes**, and each class contains **20 students**.\n- To find the total number of students, we calculate:\n  $$\n  \\text{Students initially} = 15 \\text{ classes} \\times 20 \\text{ students/class} = 300 \\text{ students}\n  $$\n\n- Next, they added **5 new classes**, each still containing **20 students**:\n  $$\n  \\text{Students added} = 5 \\text{ classes} \\times 20 \\text{ students/class} = 100 \\text{ students}\n  $$\n\n- Therefore, the total number of students is the sum of the original and new students:\n  $$\n  \\text{Total students} = 300 \\text{ students} + 100 \\text{ students} = 400 \\text{ students}\n  $$\n\n---\n\n**Final Answer:** $\\boxed{400}$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Texas Integrated School has 15 classes and has 20 students per class.  They added five more classes, how many students will they have now?", "answer": "The school has 15 x 20 = <<15*20=300>>300 students right now.\nThey have an additional 5 x 20 = <<5*20=100>>100 students after adding 5 more classes.\nTherefore, Texas Integrated School will have a total of 300+100= <<300+100=400>>400 students.\n#### 400", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Texas Integrated School has 15 classes and has 20 students per class.  They added five more classes, how many students will they have now?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["400"], "solution": "400"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 8257, 49421, 6022, 702, 220, 16, 20, 6846, 323, 702, 220, 17, 15, 4143, 817, 536, 13, 220, 2379, 3694, 4236, 803, 6846, 11, 1246, 1657, 4143, 686, 807, 614, 1431, 5267, 36842, 25, 151645, 198, 151644], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 1077, 594, 1490, 13, 576, 3491, 374, 911, 8257, 49421, 6022, 13, 2379, 614, 220, 16, 20, 6846, 323, 220, 17, 15, 4143, 817, 536, 13, 5005, 807, 3694, 4236, 803, 6846, 11, 323, 279, 3405, 374, 1246, 1657, 4143, 807, 686, 614, 1431, 13, 88190, 11, 773, 358, 1184, 311, 7071, 700, 279, 2790, 1372, 315, 4143, 1283, 7842]}
