he number\" refer to something else? Like half of half? But no, the problem states \"half the number,\" so it should be straightforward.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can set up an equation to verify. Let me assign variables. Let F = number of students in Mrs. Finley's class, which is 24. Let J = number of students in Mr. Johnson's class. According to the problem, J = 10 + (half of F). Half of F is 24/2 = 12. Then 10 + 12 = 22. Yep, so J = 22.\n\nIs there any chance that \"half the number\" could mean something else, like half of half the number? Wait, that would be half of 24, which is 12, which is the same as before. So either way, we get the same result. Therefore, the answer should be 22. \n\nI don't think there's any mistake here. The key steps are clearly identifying half of 24, adding 10, which results in 22. So, I can confidently say that Mr. Johnson's class has 22 students.\n\n**Final Answer**\nMr. Johnson's class has \\boxed{22} students.\n</think>\n\nTo determine how many students are in Mr. Johnson’s class, we start with the information provided:\n\n- Mrs. Finley’s class has **24 students**.\n\nAccording to the problem, **Mr. Johnson’s class has 10 more than half the number of students in Mrs. Finley’s class**.\n\n1. First, find half of the number of students in Mrs. Finley’s class:\n\n$$\n\\frac{24}{2} = 12\n$$\n\n2. Add 10 to this value to find the number of students in Mr. Johnson’s class:\n\n$$\n12 + 10 = 22\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Final Answer**: Mr. Johnson’s class has $\\boxed{22}$ students.<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Mrs. Finley's class has 24 students, and Mr. Johnson's class has 10 more than half the number in Mrs. Finley's class. How many students are in Mr. Johnson's class?", "answer": "Half the number in Mrs. Finley's class is 24/2=<<24/2=12>>12.\nMr. Johnson has 12+10=<<12+10=22>>22 students.\n#### 22", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Mrs. Finley's class has 24 students, and Mr. Johnson's class has 10 more than half the number in Mrs. Finley's class. How many students are in Mr. Johnson's class?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["22"], "solution": "22"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 17618, 13, 5649, 3179, 594, 536, 702, 220, 17, 19, 4143, 11, 323, 4392, 13, 11351, 594, 536, 702, 220, 16, 15, 803, 1091, 4279, 279, 1372, 304, 17618, 13, 5649, 3179, 594, 536, 13, 2585, 1657], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 773, 1077, 752, 1430, 311, 7071, 700, 419, 3491, 13, 88190, 11, 279, 3491, 2727, 429, 17618, 13, 5649, 3179, 594, 536, 702, 220, 17, 19, 4143, 11, 323, 4392, 13, 11351, 594, 536, 702, 220, 16, 15, 803, 1091, 4279, 279, 1372, 304, 17618, 13, 5649, 3179, 594, 536, 13, 1205, 1184, 311, 1477, 700, 1246, 1657, 4143, 525]}
