ms had 45, American Summits has 2x that. So, 2 * 45 = 90 bottled water. Let me write that as 90 bottles. \n\nThen, Hormel donated three times the number of dressed chickens that Foster Farms donated. Wait, so 3 times 45? Let me check. 3 * 45 is 135. So Hormel donated 135 dressed chickens. \n\nBoudin Butchers donated one-third of the number of dressed chickens that Hormel donated. So, one-third of 135. Let me calculate that. 135 divided by 3 is 45. So Boudin Butchers donated 45 dressed chickens. \n\nDel Monte Foods donated 30 fewer bottles of water than American Summits. American Summits had 90 bottles, so Del Monte has 90 - 30 = 60 bottles. \n\nNow, I need to add up all the items donated by each company and find the total. \n\nLet me list all the numbers again to make sure:\n\nFoster Farms: 45 dressed chickens\n\nAmerican Summits: 90 bottles\n\nHormel: 135 chickens\n\nBoudin Butchers: 45 chickens\n\nDel Monte: 60 bottles\n\nSo total items would be:\n\nDressed chickens: 45 + 135 + 45 = 225\n\nBottled water: 90 + 60 = 150\n\nTotal items: 225 + 150 = 375\n\nWait, let me check that again. \n\nStarting over:\n\nChicken items:\n\nFoster: 45\n\nAmerican: 45*2 = 90\n\nHormel: 45*3 = 135\n\nBoudin: 135/3 = 45\n\nTotal chickens: 45+90+135+45 = let's calculate:\n\n45 + 90 = 135\n\n135 + 135 = 270\n\n270 + 45 = 315. Wait, hold on! Earlier I said 45+135+45=225, but here it's 315. Did I miscalculate? Let me recount.\n\nFoster: 45\n\nAmerican: 90 (2x Foster)\n\nHormel: 135 (3x Foster)\n\nBoudin: (1/3) Hormel's chickens, which is 135/3=45.\n\nSo total chickens: 45 + 90 + 135 + 45 = 45*3 + 90 = 135 + 90 = 225. Wait, 45 + 90 is 135, plus 135 is 270, plus 45 is 315? Wait, hold on. Let me add them step by step:\n\nFirst, Foster and American:\n\n45 (Foster) + 90 (American) = 135.\n\nThen Hormel adds 135, so total now 135 + 135 = 270.\n\nThen Boudin adds 45, so total 270 + 45 = 315. Okay, that's 315 chicken items. \n\nBut earlier I thought of 45 + 90 + 135 + 45 = 225, which is wrong. I think I confused the order. So total chickens is 315, and bottled water is 90 + 60 = 150. 315 + 150 = 465. \n\nBut wait, let me check again. Let me list all numbers:\n\nFoster: 45\n\nAmerican: 90\n\nHormel: 135\n\nBoudin: 45\n\nDel Monte: 60\n\nTotal chicken: 45 + 90 + 135 + 45. Let me add them again:\n\n45 + 90 = 135\n\n135 + 135 = 270\n\n270 + 45 = 315. So 315 chickens.\n\nBottle water: 90 (American) + 60 (Del Monte) = 150 bottles.\n\nTotal food items: 315 + 150 = 465. \n\nBut wait, in my first calculation, I thought of the chickens as 45 + 135 + 45, which was 225, and then added the bottled water. But that was incorrect because when I added hormones first, then adding other companies, but maybe I miscalculated. \n\nSo total food is 465. Let me check once more.\n\nYes, 45 + 90 + 135 + 45 + 60 = 45*2 + 90 + 60 + 135. Wait:\n\nFoster and American: 45 + 90 = 135\n\nHormel and Boudin: 135 + 45 = 180\n\nDel Monte: 60\n\nTotal: 135 + 180 + 60 = 375? Wait, no, wait. Wait, total chicken is 315, total bottles is 150, so 315 + 150 = 465. \n\nBut if I think of adding all:\n\nFoster: 45\n\nAmerican: + 90 = 135\n\nHormel: + 135 = 270\n\nBoudin: + 45 = 315\n\nDel Monte: + 60 = 375.\n\nYes! So total is 375. \n\nWait, but earlier when I thought of 315 chickens and 150 bottles, but then I miscalculated the total? So why did I first get confused? Let me check again:\n\nTotal chicken items: 45 + 90 + 135 + 45 = 315. \n\nTotal water items: 90 + 60 = 150. \n\nTotal items: 315 + 150 = 465. \n\nBut in the solution they gave, maybe they got 465. However, let me check once more. \n\nAnother way: Let's compute each:\n\n1. Foster: 45 chickens\n\n2. American: 2*45=90\n\n3. Hormel: 3*45=135\n\n4. Boudin: 1/3 of Hormel's (135/3=45)\n\n5. Del Monte: 90 - 30 = 60\n\nTotal chicken: 45 + 90 + 135 + 45 = 315\n\nTotal water: 90 + 60 = 150\n\nTotal: 315 + 150 = 465. \n\nSo the answer should be 465. \n\nBut wait, let me check once more with another method. Maybe compute each company's items as per problem statement:\n\nFoster Farms: 45\n\nAmerican Summits: twice that = 90\n\nHormel: three times that = 135\n\nBoudin: one-third that = 45\n\nDel Monte: 30 fewer than American Summits, which is 90 - 30 = 60.\n\nYes, same numbers. So total is 465. Therefore, the answer is \\boxed{465}.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe total number of food items donated by all companies is \\boxed{465}.\n</think>\n\nTo determine the total number of food items donated by the five companies, let's break down the information systematically:\n\n- **Foster Farms** donated 45 dressed chickens.\n- **American Summits** donated **twice** the number of appetizers donated by Foster Farms.  \n  $$\n  2 \\times 45 = 90 \\text{ bottles}\n  $$\n- **Hormel** donated **three times** the number of chicken items as Foster Farms.  \n  $$\n  3 \\times 45 = 135 \\text{ dressed chickens}\n  $$\n- **Boudin Butchers** donated **one-third** of the number of dressed chickens donated by Hormel.  \n  $$\n  \\frac{1}{3} \\times 135 = 45 \\text{ dressed chickens}\n  $$\n- **Del Monte Foods** donated **30 fewer bottles** than American Summits.  \n  $$\n  90 - 30 = 60 \\text{ bottles}\n  $$\n\n**", "source_record": {"question": "Five food companies sponsored a local food bank. Foster Farms donated 45 dressed chickens; American Summits donated twice the number of bottled water than the number of dressed chicken donated by Foster Farms; Hormel donated three times the number of dressed chickens that Foster Farms donated; Boudin Butchers donated one-third of the number of dressed chickens that Hormel donated; Del Monte Foods donated 30 fewer bottles of water than American Summits. How many food items did the companies donate in total?", "answer": "American Summits donated 45 x 2 = <<45*2=90>>90 bottled waters.\nHormel donated 45 x 3 = <<45*3=135>>135 spams.\nBoudin Bakery donated 135 x 1/3 = <<135*1/3=45>>45 sourdoughs.\nDel Monte Foods donated 90 - 30 = <<90-30=60>>60 canned fruits.\nTherefore, a total of 45 + 90 + 135 + 45 + 60 