en 15 parks times 12 should be 180, yes. So 200 total minus 180 is 20. That seems right. \n\nWait, maybe I missed something. The problem says the squares don't have enough, so maybe they can't do anything with the unused ones? Well, the problem is asking how many are unused, so regardless of the situation, it's just 20. Yeah, I think that's correct. \n\nBut just to make sure, let me rephrase. Total streetlights: 200. Used: 15 parks * 12 = 180. Unused: 200 - 180 = 20. Yep. So the answer is 20. So I think that's it. \n\n**Final Answer**\nThe number of unused streetlights is \\boxed{20}.\n</think>\n\nTo determine the number of streetlights that remain unused, let's break the problem into clear, step-by-step reasoning:\n\n### Step 1: Identify the total number of streetlights\nThe council bought **200** streetlights in total.\n\n### Step 2: Determine how many will be used\n\nThere are **15 squares** in New York City. Each square will be given **12 streetlights**. Therefore, the total number of streetlights used is:\n\n$$\n15 \\times 12 = 180\n$$\n\n### Step 3: Calculate the number of unused streetlights\n\nNow subtract the used streetlights from the total:\n\n$$\n200 - 180 = 20\n$$\n\n### Final Answer\n\nThe number of unused streetlights is:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{20}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "The New York City Council bought 200 streetlights to modernize its squares. But they don't have enough squares to use all the streetlights bought. Knowing that there are 15 squares in New York and that each park will have 12 new streetlights bought by the city council, how many unused streetlights will remain?", "answer": "So the total number of streetlights that will be used is 15 squares * 12 streetlights/square = <<15*12=180>>180 streetlights\nThere will be 200 streetlights - 180 streetlights = <<200-180=20>>20 streetlights unused.\n#### 20", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: The New York City Council bought 200 streetlights to modernize its squares. But they don't have enough squares to use all the streetlights bought. Knowing that there are 15 squares in New York and that each park will have 12 new streetlights bought by the city council, how many unused streetlights will remain?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["20"], "solution": "20"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 576, 1532, 4261, 4311, 9074, 10788, 220, 17, 15, 15, 8592, 13826, 311, 6481, 551, 1181, 31340, 13, 1988, 807, 1513, 944, 614, 3322, 31340, 311, 990, 678, 279, 8592, 13826, 10788, 13, 57633, 429, 1052, 525], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 1077, 594, 1490, 13, 576, 3491, 2727, 279, 1532, 4261, 4311, 9074, 10788, 220, 17, 15, 15, 8592, 13826, 11, 714, 807, 646, 944, 990, 1105, 678, 369, 279, 220, 16, 20, 31340, 13, 8886, 6118, 686, 614, 220, 16, 17, 501, 8592, 13826, 13, 1205, 1184, 311, 1477, 700, 1246, 1657, 525, 20006, 382, 5338, 11, 358, 1184, 311]}
